\section{Implementation and reproducibility record}
\label{sec:implementation_record}
The following details specify the evaluated models and coordinate protocol. They support reproduction of the main article's results and do not introduce additional algorithmic claims.

\subsection{Body-reference selection and landmark conventions}


The two input streams may differ in global position, orientation, and scale because they are estimated independently. All experiments use the archived preprocessing of the evaluated checkpoints so that training and evaluation follow the same executable protocol. Its behavior is batch-referenced rather than sample-wise or frame-wise. For an input stream minibatch \(P\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times J\times 3}\), the implementation first flattens the batch and time dimensions in batch-major order. In the default \texttt{first\_frame} mode, the reference is therefore the first frame of the first sequence, \(P_{0,0}\), not the first frame of every sequence.

Let \(h_L\), \(h_R\), and \(n\) denote the configured left hip-root, right hip-root, and neck slots, and let \(\operatorname{nrm}(v)=v/(\|v\|_2+\epsilon)\), with \(\epsilon=10^{-8}\). The configured indices are \((h_L,h_R,n)=(6,7,14)\) for the Unity joint layout and \((4,5,12)\) for the Ski-PTZ-Pose common-13 layout. The stream-specific batch anchor and axes are
\[
c^P=\frac{1}{2}\left(P_{0,0,h_L}+P_{0,0,h_R}\right),
\]
\[
e_x^P=\operatorname{nrm}\!\left(P_{0,0,h_R}-P_{0,0,h_L}\right),
\qquad
e_y^P=\operatorname{nrm}\!\left(P_{0,0,n}-c^P\right),
\]
\[
e_z^P=\operatorname{nrm}\!\left(e_x^P\times e_y^P\right).
\]
With the default face-direction option enabled, the sign of \(e_z^P\) is reversed if necessary according to the direction from the neck to the midpoint of tensor positions 0 and 1. These positions are the two eye landmarks in the Unity 15-joint layout. In the Ski-PTZ-Pose common-13 layout, however, they are the first two retained upper-arm landmarks because the default face-direction indices are not remapped in the executable path. The column matrix \(R^P=[e_x^P,e_y^P,e_z^P]\) is then applied to every sequence and frame in that stream minibatch:
\[
\tilde{P}_{b,t,j}=\left(P_{b,t,j}-c^P\right)R^P.
\]
The left input, right input, and reference target are passed through this function separately, so they use independently estimated anchors \((c^L,R^L)\), \((c^R,R^R)\), and \((c^G,R^G)\), respectively.

This literal implementation normalizes the three constructed axis vectors but does not re-orthogonalize \(e_y^P\) after the cross product and does not divide coordinates by a skeleton scale. Thus, \(R^P\) is a landmark-derived axis matrix rather than a guaranteed orthonormal rotation, and coordinate magnitudes remain in the upstream or dataset coordinate units. The transform also depends on minibatch composition and order: changing the first sequence, batch size, or batch grouping can change the transformed coordinates of other sequences. The reported results retain the archived batching protocol so that the description and numbers correspond to the same executable method. Consequently, canonical coordinates here refer to this fixed batch anchor, not to a new pelvis origin and body orientation at every frame. Any motion retained relative to the anchor depends on the upstream coordinates; the transform does not recover a trajectory in a shared world frame.

\subsection{Similarity fit and missing inputs}
The implementation uses an unweighted SVD solution. With row means \(\bar{x},\bar{y}\), centered coordinates \(X_c,Y_c\), and \(X_c^\top Y_c/N=U\operatorname{diag}(\sigma)V^\top\), it computes
\[
\begin{aligned}
D&=\operatorname{diag}(1,1,\det(VU^\top)), & Q^\star&=VDU^\top,\\
s^\star&=\frac{\sum_{k=1}^{3}D_{kk}\sigma_k}
{\max(\|X_c\|_F^2/N,10^{-8})}, &
(d^\star)^\top&=\bar{y}-s^\star\bar{x}(Q^\star)^\top.
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus, reflection and independent scaling along coordinate axes are excluded. The same transform is applied to all frames and joints; rotation and scale are not fitted separately for each frame. Both directions are solved independently. These are auxiliary pose-space transforms, not estimated camera extrinsics, and they are distinct from the evaluation-only per-frame fit used for PA-MPJPE.

The fusion model consumes dense, finite 3D tensors without an input-validity channel. In the pose-corruption study, selected raw input entries are replaced by zero or perturbed before landmark canonicalization; the image-occlusion study likewise retains failed detections as all-zero raw poses. The corruption and detection-failure flags are used for reporting, not as network inputs or alignment weights. Every joint-frame entry, including a zero-filled or corrupted entry after transformation, participates equally in the fit above. A raw zero need not remain zero after subtracting the batch anchor. Corruption of the landmarks that define that anchor can also affect other sequences in the same minibatch. The evaluated fusion path neither interpolates missing joints nor excludes them from alignment. The reference pose is not corrupted. The training-objective subsection of the main article specifies how the same absence of input-validity weighting applies to training.

\subsection{Feature boundary conventions}
Each joint-frame feature concatenates five three-dimensional cues: own-view pose, aligned opposite-view pose, their difference, temporal velocity, and the alignment rotation vector. Velocity is the first finite difference of the canonical pose, with zero velocity at the first frame. The sequence-level rotation vector is repeated across all joints and frames of that sequence. The same shared encoder is applied to the two view directions.

\subsection{Training and comparison models}


Within each dataset, all methods operate on the same synchronized left- and right-view pose sequences and joint convention: the full 15-joint CanonFuse3D skeleton for Unity and the common 13-joint subset for Ski-PTZ-Pose. The input to the proposed model is a tensor of joint-frame pose features in the transformed coordinate representation; no RGB frames, 2D keypoints, masks, camera intrinsics, or camera extrinsics are provided to the refinement network. Left and right streams are canonicalized in separate calls, each using the first sequence in the current minibatch as its anchor, and are then aligned in both directions to form the auxiliary opposite-view poses and residual features described in the proposed-method section of the main article.

During training, the reference tensor is transformed in a third, independent canonicalization call: the Unity avatar skeleton is used for the synthetic dataset and the fused reference pose is used for Ski-PTZ-Pose. Thus, Ski-PTZ-Pose training targets the constructed reference under its own batch anchor rather than independent physical ground truth. The author-collected videos are not included in either training path and do not affect checkpoint or hyperparameter selection. For each indexed camera pair, the frames shared by both streams and the reference are uniformly resampled to a 30-frame sequence; the index entries are not sliding windows.

Both dataset-specific models use a hidden dimension of 128, four attention heads, dropout 0.1, a depthwise temporal-convolution kernel of size 5, AdamW with learning rate \(10^{-3}\) and weight decay 0.01, and a 100-epoch training budget. The loss weights are \(\lambda_{view}=0.05\), \(\lambda_{bal}=0.02\), \(\lambda_{ent}=0.005\), and \(\lambda_{temp}=0.01\). The Unity reference checkpoint, used for every controlled Unity evaluation, is the stored epoch-89 checkpoint trained with batch size 4096. All frozen Unity evaluations use the archived batch size of 256; because the executable first-frame transformation is batch-referenced, this evaluation batch size is part of the numerical protocol.

The reported Ski-PTZ-Pose evaluation uses a separate dataset-specific \texttt{last.ckpt} from epoch 97, trained with batch size 4 on the common 13-joint representation. The archived Ski index contains 150 training, zero validation, and 30 test camera-pair entries. The historical training path reused the training list for epoch monitoring because the index has no non-empty validation partition; those monitoring values are therefore not treated as independent validation evidence. To avoid retrospective test-based selection, we fix \texttt{last.ckpt} in advance and evaluate all 30 test entries once with \texttt{drop\_last=False}. The checkpoint and index SHA-256 digests are recorded in the evidence manifest.


The executed Unity index uses an action-disjoint train--validation--test split containing 12, eight, and four avatar-specific action sequences, respectively. Pairing all 180 viewpoints yields 193,320 training, 128,880 validation, and 64,440 test camera-pair sequences. We keep the complete final test batch (\texttt{drop\_last=False}) and seed all perturbation generators with 42. The complete-split Unity record supplies the primary result and all controlled robustness, generalization, adaptation, and N-view analyses. The Ski-PTZ-Pose record is an independent journal re-evaluation under its fixed common-13 test index and dataset-specific checkpoint. The two datasets use different reference regimes and are never pooled; all comparisons are made within a dataset and representation.


The direct comparison group uses the same native 3D pose streams, joint subset, synchronization, and evaluation protocol as CanonFuse3D. The additional-input geometric group uses the same indexed sequences and frames with estimated 2D observations and calibration. Raw-space single-view and averaging results remain in the archived evidence; main-table errors are measured after the stated body-coordinate transformation.

\paragraph{Expanded pose-only baselines.}
The direct-input groups in the main Unity and Ski-PTZ-Pose comparison tables include both single views, equal canonical averaging, sequence-level right-to-left similarity alignment followed by equal averaging, and a 3D quality-weighted average. The latter assigns one weight to each view and sequence using temporal second-difference magnitude and temporal bone-length inconsistency, each normalized by median bone length; it uses neither detection confidence nor reference poses. A collapsed skeleton is rejected when the other view is non-degenerate. These are controlled geometric and heuristic baselines rather than published learned fusion systems.

We additionally train a framewise cross-view MLP, a temporal convolutional fusion baseline, and canonical averaging followed by SmoothNet (cited in the main article). The MLP concatenates both complete poses and predicts an additive residual through two 256-unit ReLU layers with dropout 0.1. The TCN uses concatenated pose channels, two 128-channel hidden convolutions, and an output convolution; all three kernels have width 5, with dropout 0.1 in the hidden layers. SmoothNet retains the official 512-unit encoder, five 512--128--512 residual blocks, dropout 0.25, and temporal decoder, with its input and output windows adapted to the same 30 sampled frames. It refines the average pose with shared temporal weights across joint coordinates. The MLP and TCN are local controlled architectures; SmoothNet is an adaptation of the published temporal refinement method, not a competing multi-view reconstruction network.

\paragraph{Additional-input geometric references.}
A separate group uses the native SAM3D estimated 2D joints and supplied camera calibration to triangulate corresponding observations by DLT. The robust variant rejects joint-frame estimates whose maximum two-view reprojection residual exceeds 25 pixels and fills gaps by temporal interpolation of accepted estimates, with nearest-value extension at sequence boundaries. Tracks with no accepted point retain their finite ungated DLT estimates; validity and fallback counts are archived. Every fixed test sample is retained. The DLT-residual MLP predicts a correction from the complete canonicalized DLT pose, log-scaled reprojection residuals, and validity indicators, using two 256-unit hidden layers. No annotation coordinates are supplied as predictive features. All three methods are evaluated after independently applying the same body-coordinate transformation to their predictions and to the reference. Ski-PTZ-Pose annotations are pelvis-relative in every frame: the source hip joint and the midpoint of the two hip joints are zero. We therefore first subtract each geometric prediction's own per-frame hip midpoint (common-13 joints 4 and 5) from its joints, removing world translation without using reference coordinates; Unity retains its world-coordinate trajectory. The subsequent batch-referenced body transform is unchanged. The prediction preprocessing fits no scale or additional similarity transform to test annotations. The separately reported PA-MPJPE uses an evaluation-only reference alignment for every method, as defined in the metrics subsection. This group therefore has additional input requirements and does not establish a common-input ranking against the pose-only methods.

\paragraph{Baseline training and selection.}
All added learned baselines use seed 42, AdamW with learning rate $10^{-3}$ and weight decay 0.01, and a fixed 100-epoch budget. The residual MLP and TCN objectives combine position L1 error with weight 0.01 on predicted second-difference magnitude. SmoothNet uses its position L1 objective plus weight 0.1 on acceleration L1 error. Unity uses the complete action-disjoint training and validation indices, batch size 4096 for training and validation, and the checkpoint with lowest validation MPJPE on all 15 joints; evaluation uses batch size 256. SmoothNet accumulates gradients over microbatches of 2048 after the complete 4096-sequence batch has been canonicalized, preserving the batch anchor. The archived Unity CanonFuse3D \texttt{last.ckpt} has weights identical to the stored epoch-89 validation-selected checkpoint.

The Ski training index contains 150 entries, of which the original loader admits 145: five pairs involving camera 0 in subject 2, sequence 202 have no surviving shared frames. All added Ski models use these same 145 eligible training entries, batch size 4, and the fixed final epoch-99 checkpoint; no test metric selects their parameters or stopping point. Training follows the archived shuffled, complete-batch policy, processing 192,512 Unity or 144 Ski entries per epoch, with the omitted entries changing with the seeded shuffle. Validation and test retain their final partial batches. CanonFuse3D retains its predeclared dataset-specific checkpoint, so these are single-run comparisons under stated training budgets, not averages over independent training seeds.


\section{Controlled evaluation details}
\label{sec:controlled_details}
To evaluate robustness, we mask the input 3D pose streams to simulate missing or unreliable joints caused by occlusion, tracking failure, or upstream pose-estimation errors. We test three masking protocols. Random joint-frame masking independently removes sampled joints at individual frames. Distal-subset masking restricts corruption to the same eight filtered joint indices used by the archived executable protocol. Temporal masking removes contiguous short frame intervals to simulate burst occlusion or tracking failure. We compare the proposed model with canonical average fusion under the same corrupted inputs.

We separately test image-level occlusion by modifying the Unity RGB frames before SAM 3D Body inference. Ground-truth projected 2D joints are used only to place the synthetic occluders; neither those coordinates nor their visibility flags are passed to SAM 3D Body or CanonFuse3D. A selected joint is covered by a square filled with the rounded per-image mean RGB value. Its side length is 12\% of the projected person height, with a minimum of 16 pixels, and the square is clipped to the image boundary. The random protocol selects every joint independently in every source frame, the distal protocol applies the same rule only to the eight archived distal-joint indices, and the temporal protocol selects a fixed fraction of the 15 joints per camera stream and covers each selected joint for one deterministically positioned 10-frame interval. Failed person detections are retained as zero poses with an explicit failure flag rather than removed from evaluation. Detection failure rates average these flags over the affected views after alignment to the fixed camera-pair sequence index, pooling both views for bilateral interventions. They therefore reflect evaluation-frame occurrences rather than unique source-image counts.

\subsection{Perturbation, transfer, and view-group protocols}


Every frozen-checkpoint analysis loads the same reference checkpoint (identified in the accompanying evidence manifest) and evaluates all 64,440 camera-pair sequences in the held-out Unity test split. The temporal-offset, sampling-rate, view-angle, and masking experiments use all 15 CanonFuse3D joints. The front-end comparison uses the common 13-joint subset (indices 2--14) shared by all upstream estimators and the model output. Reported errors are in dataset coordinate units after the batch-referenced transform; no conversion to millimeters is applied. The canonicalization code performs no skeleton-scale division, although the subsequent sequence-level similarity alignment can estimate relative scale between the two input streams.

The temporal study shifts the right pose stream using seven offsets from \(-2\) to \(+2\) frames, including half-frame conditions, with linear interpolation and boundary replication. The sampling study uses seven local clock-rate errors from \(-2\%\) to \(+2\%\) around the center frame. Camera-pair geometry is grouped by circular azimuth separation into six 30-degree bins. The pose-stream masking grid crosses three affected-view modes (left, right, both), three patterns (independent random joint-frame, distal-joint, and contiguous temporal), and five ratios (0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0). The image-level study uses the same three affected-view modes and pattern names at ratios 0.5 and 1.0, giving 18 evaluation cells. Six deterministic front-end prediction sets cover 720 camera streams and 17,089 unique RGB frames each; the resulting camera streams are paired according to the unchanged 64,440-sequence test index. Image- and pose-level ratios govern different interventions and are therefore compared as matched protocol labels, not as equal physical occlusion severity. Image-level ratio 1.0 selects all eligible joints under the corresponding pattern; it does not imply that all image pixels are covered.

For front-end transfer, VideoPose3D, PoseFormer, and MotionBERT lift the same Unity ground-truth H36M-17 2D tracks. This deliberately isolates the 2D-to-3D lifting stage rather than comparing image detectors. Outputs are remapped to the CanonFuse3D convention before fusion, and estimator repository commits, checkpoint hashes, manifest hashes, sample counts, and coordinate conventions are recorded in the accompanying evidence manifest.

The following experiments characterize deployment beyond the native two-view, native-front-end condition without changing the CanonFuse3D architecture. Front-end adaptation uses the same fixed Unity train--validation--test split and the common 13-joint subset. VideoPose3D, PoseFormer, and MotionBERT each define two adaptation scopes: the gate and residual heads only, or all CanonFuse3D parameters. A seventh model is fully adapted on a balanced mixture of the native SAM3D stream and all three alternative front ends. Every adaptation uses the training and validation actions only, runs for 20 epochs with learning rate \(10^{-4}\), and selects the checkpoint by validation common-13 MPJPE. The frozen reference checkpoint plus seven adapted models are evaluated on all four front ends, producing an \(8\times4\) matrix of 32 test cells without test-set adaptation.

For N-view scaling, a camera group is proposed from each complete 36-camera action ring by cyclic de-duplication, yielding 180 proposed groups before data-quality filtering. Each accepted group must provide at least 30 frame indices shared by all four cameras. The nested inputs use one, two, three, and four cameras from the same group. Because CanonFuse3D is a dual-view model, the N-view prediction is formed only by composing all unique pairwise forward passes. Canonical averaging provides the non-learned baseline, the mean of pairwise CanonFuse3D predictions is the deployable aggregation, and selection of the best complete pair is reported separately as an oracle upper bound.

For the efficiency analysis, inputs are transferred to the GPU before timing. We execute ten four-view warm-up compositions, synchronize CUDA before and after each timed region, and then measure each of the 44 accepted groups once for (N=2,3,4). The timed serial path includes per-pair canonicalization, all unique pair forwards, and final arithmetic averaging; it excludes data loading, monocular front-end inference, and oracle selection. We report mean, median, and 95th-percentile latency, throughput, and peak allocated GPU memory on one NVIDIA RTX A6000 using 30-frame sequences.

The automatic synchronization experiment is an alignment-only module placed before the frozen CanonFuse3D checkpoint. For its canonical joint-velocity descriptor, every sample and view is transformed independently using that sample's own first frame; this prevents one batch element from defining another element's coordinate system. The module searches right-stream corrections from \(-5\) to \(+5\) frames in half-frame increments. Its confidence threshold is selected exclusively on the validation split by minimum offset MAE and is then frozen. Test offsets are injected at \(-5,-3,-2,-1,-0.5,0,0.5,1,2,3,\text{ and }5\) frames. All three conditions are scored on the same fixed central 20-frame interval, leaving a five-frame margin on each side, so an injected shift cannot change the evaluated pose content. The uncorrected condition uses the shifted stream, automatic uses the accepted estimated correction, and oracle restores the original unperturbed right stream. Exact lag accuracy requires absolute error below 0.25 frames; the auxiliary half-frame criterion is strict, with absolute error below 0.5 frames. A batch-order invariance test is part of the evaluation regression suite.


